WEBVTT

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:10.412
Multiply two 1024 by 1024 tables with the plain one-thread-per-answer program on a

00:00:10.412 --> 00:00:11.357
GTX 1650.

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:16.425
Is the time set by the arithmetic or by memory, and by roughly how many times?

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:17.901
Pause and write it.

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:31.085
Matrix multiplication is the calculation underneath most of modern AI.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:37.153
It is famous as the job GPUs are best at, because it has a huge amount of

00:00:37.153 --> 00:00:43.023
arithmetic: for two square tables of 1024 by 1024 numbers, over two billion

00:00:43.023 --> 00:00:45.371
multiplications and additions.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:50.825
Every GPU course then shows the same sequence: a plain version where each thread

00:00:50.825 --> 00:00:55.994
computes a single answer, a tiled version that copies small squares of the tables

00:00:55.994 --> 00:01:00.461
into fast memory inside the chip, and then faster and faster versions.

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:02.410
People memorize the sequence.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:07.197
Yet not a single multiplication is removed from one version to the next.

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:12.243
So what is every faster version actually changing, and how far does it have to go?

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:22.291
Tables A, B and the answer C are n by n.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:28.742
Each is stored as an array, with down-number i and right-number j at place i × n +

00:01:28.742 --> 00:01:28.891
j.

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:34.168
The answer at (i, j) is the sum over k of A at (i, k) times B at (k, j).

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:39.732
The A values with down-number i, a left-to-right strip of A, meet the B values with

00:01:39.732 --> 00:01:42.307
right-number j, an up-to-down strip of B.

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:48.427
In English: this is a GPU function receiving the addresses of tables A, B and C in

00:01:48.427 --> 00:01:50.959
the card's memory, and their size n.

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:56.070
Each thread computes its down-number i from its block's and its own numbers in the

00:01:56.070 --> 00:02:00.249
up-down direction, and its right-number j from the left-right direction.

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:02.574
It makes a total s holding 0.

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:08.462
For k from 0 to n minus 1, it reads A at (i, k) and B at (k, j) from memory,

00:02:08.462 --> 00:02:11.591
multiplies them, and adds the product to s.

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:15.247
Finally it stores the total as the answer at (i, j).

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:35.488
There are 1,073,741,824 multiply-adds, so 2,147,483,648 operations.

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:50.790
The minimum is 12,582,912 bytes, which allows 170.7 operations per byte.

00:02:57.140 --> 00:03:07.359
That is 8,589,934,592 bytes, only 0.25 operations per byte.

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:12.979
These are published GTX 1650 specs and an assumed clock, not a measurement.

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:14.098
Pause.

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:24.631
The units can do 2.688 trillion operations a second.

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:32.094
The memory time is 67.1 milliseconds and the arithmetic time 0.799, so the plain

00:03:32.094 --> 00:03:35.561
program is 84 times on the memory side.

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:41.403
Hardware fact: each SM has shared memory, storage inside the SM that all threads of

00:03:41.403 --> 00:03:45.638
a block can read many times faster than the card's memory chips.

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:49.467
Turing allows at most 64 KB of it per SM.

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:55.255
Tiling works like this: a block of T by T threads copies a T by T square of A and

00:03:55.255 --> 00:04:00.421
one of B into shared memory, and every value copied is then used by T threads

00:04:00.421 --> 00:04:03.239
instead of being read T times from memory.

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:04.358
Pause.

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:09.244
With T = 16: bytes, memory time, and operations per byte.

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:29.573
The bytes fall to 8,589,934,592 / 16 = 536,870,912.

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:34.464
That takes 4.19 milliseconds and gives 4 operations per byte.

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:43.188
T must equal the ratio, 84.

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:53.450
Two 84 by 84 squares need 56,448 bytes, which is 55.1 KB of an SM's 64.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:56.368
Pause.

00:04:56.720 --> 00:04:57.936
Fill the top line.

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:03.145
Then write a sentence on what every faster version changes, how far it must go, and

00:05:03.145 --> 00:05:07.357
what stops square tiles in shared memory from getting there comfortably.

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:16.367
The card needs 21 operations per byte.

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:23.202
Every faster version raises how many times each byte fetched from memory gets used:

00:05:23.202 --> 00:05:29.607
from 0.25 in the plain program toward the 21 needed, while the matrix itself would

00:05:29.607 --> 00:05:30.544
allow 170.7.

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:34.505
Square tiles alone need T = 84 to get there.

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:42.571
At T = 84 one block takes 55 of an SM's 64 KB, so no second block fits on the SM to

00:05:42.571 --> 00:05:45.175
cover the first one's waits.

00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:50.416
Faster versions therefore also reuse values held in each thread's registers, not

00:05:50.416 --> 00:05:51.759
only in shared memory.

00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:57.222
The tempting answer is "matrix multiplication is arithmetic-heavy, so the arithmetic

00:05:57.222 --> 00:05:57.891
limits it".

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:02.507
It fails because the plain program is 84 times on the memory side.

00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:10.023
Multiplying large tables has plenty of arithmetic, but a simple program fetches the

00:06:10.023 --> 00:06:15.331
same numbers from memory over and over, and the fetching, not the arithmetic, sets

00:06:15.331 --> 00:06:15.914
the time.

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:20.943
Each well-known speedup is a way to fetch a number once and use it many times while

00:06:20.943 --> 00:06:23.139
it is close to the arithmetic circuits.

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:28.442
For a manager: when a team says a model "is compute-bound", ask for the number of

00:06:28.442 --> 00:06:30.765
operations per byte of memory traffic.

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:33.819
Below about 21 on this card, it is not.

00:06:35.970 --> 00:06:38.914
Memory sets the time, by about 84 times.

00:06:39.260 --> 00:06:42.460
That is a model from published specs that ignores caches.

00:06:42.810 --> 00:06:43.855
Mark your tally.

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:52.550
The GPU has caches, so the measured plain-program time is far below 67 milliseconds.

00:06:52.900 --> 00:06:56.953
How would you show, with a measurement, how much reuse the caches provided?

00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:58.068
Pause.

00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:09.890
Measure the bytes actually read from the card's memory with the profiler: Nsight

00:07:09.890 --> 00:07:12.420
Compute, metric dram__bytes_read.sum.

00:07:12.770 --> 00:07:19.390
Compare that with 8.59 billion bytes, the no-reuse case, and 12.6 million, perfect

00:07:19.390 --> 00:07:19.874
reuse.

00:07:20.230 --> 00:07:25.549
Operations divided by measured bytes gives the operations per byte the program

00:07:25.549 --> 00:07:28.209
really achieved, to set against the 21.

